course: dictionary
lesson: 11732
title: Avoidance: keeping away
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Avoidance is a noun. It comes from the verb avoid, and it means keeping away from something or not doing something. «Her avoidance of the dog meant she never went near the park.» The word tells you the girl kept away from the dog.
explain: Avoidance can be a good thing. «Avoidance of sugar helps keep your teeth healthy.» The child chooses not to eat sugar, so the teeth stay safe.
explain: Animals use avoidance too. When a mouse hides from a cat, it shows predator avoidance: it keeps away from the animal that wants to eat it. Look for the word «of» after avoidance, because it often tells you what is being kept away from.
fact: Avoidance is a noun, and it comes from the verb avoid.
fact: Avoidance is uncountable, so we say «avoidance of danger», not «avoidances».
fact: «Avoidance» means keeping away from something or not doing something.
fact: «Predator avoidance» means an animal keeping away from the animals that hunt it.
fact: Common phrases use «of» after the word: «avoidance of danger», «avoidance of sugar».
exercise: «Wearing a helmet helps with avoidance of danger when cycling.» What does avoidance mean here? | Buying a new bike | Going to sleep on the road | *Keeping away from danger | Riding very fast on a bike
exercise: Which word is the noun for the act of keeping away from something? | careful | avoid | *avoidance | dangerous
exercise: A mouse runs into its hole when a cat comes near. What is this an example of? | *Predator avoidance | Sugar avoidance | A mouse party | Tax avoidance
test: «Her avoidance of loud noises made the fireworks hard to watch.» What does avoidance mean here? | Counting the fireworks | Loving loud noises | *Keeping away from loud noises | Making a loud noise
test: Which phrase means an animal keeping away from the animals that want to eat it? | Tax avoidance | Sugar avoidance | Avoidance of a nap | *Predator avoidance
exam: Mia says avoidance is a verb because it looks like avoid. Is she right? | No, it is a country | Yes, it is a verb | *No, it is a noun made from the verb avoid | Yes, it is an adjective
